WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.840
M3GAN's "killer dance moves" may&nbsp;
have captivated all of the internet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:08.220
but the quirky scene wasn't included&nbsp;
in the original script. Here's why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:13.560
director Gerard Johnstone chose to sneak in the&nbsp;
made-for-viral moment into the final cut of M3GAN.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:18.120
Released in January 2023, M3GAN&nbsp;
tells the story of a robotics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:22.740
engineer who uses a lifelike artificial&nbsp;
intelligence doll to befriend her niece,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:27.660
only for the doll to get carried away with&nbsp;
its overprotective nature, embarking on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:32.460
killing spree. With an engaging premise, relevant&nbsp;
commentary on our relationship with technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:37.320
and horror master James Wan co-writing&nbsp;
the story and acting as a producer, M3GAN&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:42.573
garnered a good amount of hype from movie fans&nbsp;
who expected nothing less than a campy good time.

00:00:42.573 --> 00:00:43.773
"What are you?"

00:00:43.773 --> 00:00:44.520
"I'm M3GAN."

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:49.080
As exciting as the premise is and the talent&nbsp;
involved, what really got people interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:54.360
is a viral moment that has dominated social&nbsp;
media for months. A key moment from the film's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:59.040
trailer received the most attention, going on&nbsp;
to practically define the film. The marketing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:03.540
for M3GAN features the titular uncanny doll&nbsp;
performing an elaborate dance before killing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:08.940
her next victim. The dance became an online&nbsp;
sensation, spreading throughout social media like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:13.980
wildfire. An instant meme, the dance sequence's&nbsp;
absurdity has taken on a life of its own.

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:19.140
It may seem that the now-iconic moment is nothing&nbsp;
more but a hilariously out-of-place moment in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:24.360
otherwise seemingly serious film, but director&nbsp;
Gerard Johnstone and the team behind the Blumhouse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:29.580
pic are more into the joke than audiences might&nbsp;
expect. Busting a move before committing murder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:33.840
may not be a traditional horror scene, but&nbsp;
it was nevertheless an idea that the team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.580
behind M3GAN chose to run with. The widespread&nbsp;
appeal of the quirky moment has not only helped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:43.080
further spread the word of the horror film,&nbsp;
but has thoroughly pleased all those involved.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:47.280
In an interview with toofab, director&nbsp;
Johnstone admitted that he "just kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.620
of snuck" the scene into the film and is glad that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:53.394
it went over so well with audiences.&nbsp;
